    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm having some issues with my sourdough starter stability. Lately the pH has been dropping way faster than usual and the acidity is spiking inconsistently, which is messing with my dough's rise and overall crumb structure. I've been sticking to a 20% rye addition, but I'm wondering how much of this could be attributed to fluctuations in ambient temperature vs. the protein content of my base flour? Any insight on how to stabilize the microbial population?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The stability of sourdough starter can indeed be influenced by both rye content and ambient temperature. Rye is higher in mineral content and can consume more lytic acid, thus contributing to more variable acidity levels. Conversely, colder temperatures slow down yeast and lactic acid bacteria activity, which can lead to less gas production and hence unexpectedly lower pH levels. To stabilize your starter, try maintaining a consistent temperature range (ideally 68-75°F / 20-24°C) and regular feeding schedule (every 24 hours), and consider slightly reducing the rye content if you're noticing extreme variations in pH levels. Additionally, ensure even hydration and room for gas expansion by making frequent small feedings. If the microbial population continues to be unstable, you might also try introducing a flour like equal parts whole wheat and rye to diversify the microorganisms and stabilize the dough's structure.",
      "source": "target",
